# The INPUT of requirements.lock. Edit this file, never the lock.
#
#   python3 scripts/compile_locks.py            regenerate every hashed lock
#   python3 scripts/compile_locks.py --check    what CI runs
#
# EVERY LINE IS AN EXACT PIN, the transitives included. pip-compile resolves
# from what is written here, so a newer release upstream cannot move a
# version: a compile can only add or change hashes. A package the compile
# needs and this file does not pin is refused, and the refusal prints the
# line to add.
#
# `requirements.lock` is what `make install`, the README, the deploy docs and
# the Dockerfile install, and it is the SUBJECT OF THE SCA GATE: `pip-audit`
# reads it (.github/workflows/ci.yml, the "SCA" step). Every package that
# ships is named there with its hashes, or that package ships and is never
# audited. `tests/test_ci_executes_nothing_it_did_not_pin.py` pins that
# containment for the image.
#
# NOT COVERED, stated rather than implied: the dev/test packages in
# `requirements-ci.in` that are not here (pytest and friends, ruff, mypy,
# bandit, hypothesis, pip-audit, pyjwt) are installed in CI only. They do not
# ship. The nightly workflow audits them; the pull-request gate does not.

# ── What bot/ and api_bridge import, or ship, by name ───────────────────────
python-dotenv==1.2.2
pydantic==2.13.3
ccxt==4.5.56
python-telegram-bot==22.7
openai==2.38.0
anthropic==0.104.1
numpy==2.3.5
aiohttp==3.14.4
# Not imported by name. ccxt, httpx, openai and anthropic pull it in, and
# the lock is the only file the pull-request audit reads, so an unpinned
# certifi was shipped and never scanned. 2026.10.19 drops the withdrawn
# emSign roots (PYSEC-2026-4320).
certifi==2026.10.19
websockets==16.0
cryptography==50.0.0
# ── Added 2026-08-17, after cards stopped rendering on a fresh venv ─────────
# The lock had ten pins and bot/ imports twenty modules. A venv built from it
# was missing Pillow, so `bot/formatters/signal_card.py` took its
# `except ImportError` branch, returned b"", and the caller's `if png:` sent
# text instead — no card, no error, one log line. The bot looked healthy.
# `tests/test_requirements_cover_imports.py` now refuses that gap.
#
# Union of two independent fixes for the same incident: `uvicorn` came from
# main (fastapi needs a server to be worth pinning), `redis` from this branch
# (bot/api/token_store.py imports it bare). Neither list was wrong; each was
# missing what the other found, which is its own argument for the guard.
Pillow==13.0.0
fastapi==0.141.1
# The `standard` extra, since 2026-11-19. The lock said plain `uvicorn` while
# the Makefile, the Dockerfile, pyproject.toml and the CI set all installed
# `uvicorn[standard]`, on an install line of their own that no lock and no
# audit covered. Same uvicorn; the four packages the extra adds (httptools,
# uvloop, watchfiles, pyyaml) are pinned with the transitives below.
uvicorn[standard]==0.52.3
redis==8.1.0
# ── Added 2026-08-17, same incident, one layer down ─────────────────────────
# Cards came back and charts did not. `bot/skills/chart_renderer.py` guards
# its imports the same way signal_card.py guards Pillow, and returns None on
# the missing-libs path, so /chart sent text and logged one line.
#
# These three were listed in tests/optional_imports.json with the note "not
# installed in any environment today, so the chart path has never run. Pin it
# or delete the module — but that is a product decision, not a lockfile one."
# The decision has now been made: charts are a shipped feature. All 20 tests
# in tests/test_chart_renderer.py pass against these versions — they had never
# executed before, because the whole file was skipif'd on the libs it needed.
matplotlib==3.11.1
mplfinance==0.12.10b0
pandas==3.0.5
# mplfinance/_utils.py does `from six.moves import zip`. python-dateutil
# 4.0.0, the transitive pandas resolves, no longer depends on six, so a fresh
# install of the three pins above could not import mplfinance (CI,
# 2026-11-05).
six==1.17.0

# ── Transitives ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Frozen on 2026-11-19 at what a fresh Python 3.11 install of the pins above
# resolved that day, which is what CI was installing. Until then none of
# these was pinned anywhere: each install took whatever was newest. The
# production venv was not read when this list was written.
#
# A pin here that nothing requires any more is refused by
# tests/test_the_lock_is_hash_pinned.py, except the ones named there with a
# reason.
